<section id="membership">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">The plan · why membership</p>
    <h2>Built on the<br><span style="color:var(--sage)">forever relationship.</span></h2>
    <p class="lede">The strongest personal-growth companies no longer sell one course at a time. They invite people into a membership and keep earning their trust, month after month. The research on why is consistent, and it comes from more than one place.</p>
    <div class="grid3" style="margin-top:36px">
      <div class="card"><div class="num">11%</div><p>Subscription companies grew revenue 11% faster than the S&amp;P 500 over two years, and 68% of U.S. consumers signed up for a new subscription in 2024.<br><span class="cite">Zuora, Subscription Economy Index, 2025</span></p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="num">25 to 95%</div><p>A 5% rise in customer retention lifts profit by 25% to 95%. Keeping a customer costs a fraction of finding a new one.<br><span class="cite">Frederick Reichheld, Bain &amp; Company, in Harvard Business Review</span></p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="num">92%</div><p>Twelve-month retention on annual plans, against 68% on monthly plans. Annual members stay, and failed payments almost disappear.<br><span class="cite">Baremetrics, subscription benchmarks</span></p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="grid3" style="margin-top:20px">
      <div class="card"><div class="num">$51B</div><p>The personal development market in 2025, projected near $80B by 2033.<br><span class="cite">Grand View Research</span></p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="num">122,974</div><p>Coaches worldwide, up 15% since 2023, in a $5.34B coaching industry.<br><span class="cite">ICF Global Coaching Study, 2025, with PwC</span></p><p class="pop">In an industry this crowded, Vault Human stands apart: one guided year, one method, a real coach.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="num">Under 5%</div><p>Monthly churn for memberships that mix content, community and live calls, against 8% to 12% for a course library alone. The coach is what keeps people.<br><span class="cite">Kourses, member retention benchmarks, 2026</span></p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="grid2" style="margin-top:20px">
      <div class="card"><h3>What the books say</h3><p>Robbie Kellman Baxter calls it the <i>forever transaction</i> in <i>The Membership Economy</i>: a relationship, not a sale. Tien Tzuo, who built Zuora, shows in <i>Subscribed</i> why recurring relationships beat one-time purchases. John Warrillow, in <i>The Automatic Customer</i>, shows how recurring revenue makes a company itself more valuable.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h3>The closest working model</h3><p>Mindvalley, a global personal-growth membership: every program, community and author sessions for $99 a month or $499 a year, with a tier that adds live sessions with its founder. Brendon Burchard's GrowthDay runs a daily-coaching membership on the same idea.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card" style="margin-top:20px;border-left:3px solid var(--sage)"><p style="color:var(--ink)"><b>Where Vault Human is different:</b> a single guided year instead of a library to browse, a real coach inside every membership, and a capture tool that turns each day's practice into a record of who you are becoming. Nobody else walks a person through their past, present and future in one year, with one method and one relationship. It is built to leave people bigger than it found them, and that is what people come back for.</p></div>
    <div class="card cap-card" style="margin-top:20px"><h3 style="color:var(--now)">What it could be worth</h3><p>Memberships like Mindvalley and GrowthDay are valued on recurring revenue, and businesses built this way get bought. Vault Human adds something they lack: a complete method, the keys, the chambers and the coaching patterns, that could one day help train the AI coaches and companions a modernizing world will want. That makes it a candidate for a capital event, from the multi-millions upward.</p><p class="calc">A simple way to size it: subscription businesses commonly change hands at 3 to 6 times annual recurring revenue. At year five's $2.1 million, that is roughly $6 to $13 million. At 20,000 members, about $24 million a year, the same multiples reach $70 to $140 million. Illustrative, not a promise.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="alt" id="paths">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">The plan · how people arrive</p>
    <h2>Every path runs<br>through the membership.</h2>
    <p class="lede">However someone finds us, they begin as a member. Deeper coaching is added on top, and the membership keeps renewing underneath.</p>
